Chapter: StarMapWasm
  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites Starmap chapter StarMapScene

 Browser entry point for the star map. The catalogue is not built here: the
 page hands over data/starmap.dat and this chapter reads it in place, so the
 module carries 117,931 stars without allocating one object per star.

 We say:

Section: The File Format

 starmap.dat is ours but its layout is a wire format between three programs
 (the importer, this chapter, and the page), so the offsets are written down
 once here and every reader takes them from this section.

 Header, at the base:
   0  magic "STAR"   4  version   8  star count   12 named count
   16 name table     20 dso count 24 dso offset   28 con count
   32 con offset     36 con lines

 THE SHIPPED FILE'S CONSTELLATION FIELDS ARE WRONG and this chapter does not
 read them. import-hyg.ps1 patched the constellation offset into byte 28,
 which is the COUNT's slot, so every file it has written carries an offset
 where the count belongs and a zero where the offset belongs. The importer is
 fixed; the file in the depot cannot be regenerated without hyg_v42.csv, which
 is not in the tree.

  sm-hdr-magic : Integer = 0

  sm-hdr-version : Integer = 4

  sm-hdr-stars : Integer = 8

  sm-hdr-named : Integer = 12

  sm-hdr-nameoff : Integer = 16

  sm-hdr-dsocount : Integer = 20

  sm-hdr-dsooff : Integer = 24

  sm-hdr-size : Integer = 64

 One star record is 32 bytes. Magnitude, absolute magnitude and the colour
 index are 16-bit and share words with their neighbours, which is why they are
 read as words and split rather than loaded at their own widths: the wasm plug
 has a poke-16 arm and NO peek-16 arm.

  sm-rec-size : Integer = 32

  sm-rec-x : Integer = 4

  sm-rec-y : Integer = 8

  sm-rec-z : Integer = 12

  sm-rec-magword : Integer = 16

Section: Memory Map

 The page writes the file into the window at sm-dat-base, so the window has to
 clear the data segment (heap_start measured at about 72 KB in the modules we
 ship) and the heap has to start above everything. A file larger than the
 window is REFUSED rather than allowed to run into the selection buffer.

  sm-cell-stars : Integer = 256

  sm-cell-visible : Integer = 260

  sm-cell-ready : Integer = 264

  sm-cell-datlen : Integer = 268

  sm-cell-named : Integer = 272

  sm-cell-dsocount : Integer = 276

  sm-cell-dsooff : Integer = 280

  sm-cell-nameoff : Integer = 284

  sm-cell-error : Integer = 288

  sm-cam-state : Integer = 352

  sm-dat-base : Integer = 1048576

  sm-dat-max : Integer = 5242880

  sm-visible-buf : Integer = 6291456

  sm-visible-cap : Integer = 30000

  sm-heap-floor : Integer = 8388608

 65535 is the absent marker. It is above every count a caller can read back,
 and a read zero-extends, so a negative marker is unreachable.

  sm-absent : Integer = 65535

Section: Word Access

 peek-32 zero-extends, so a value stored negative reads back as its unsigned
 image; sm-signed is the correction, and a page needs none because an
 Int32Array is signed already. sm-i16 is the same correction one width down,
 for a field that shares its word with the next one.

  sm-signed : Integer -> Integer
  sm-signed (u) = if u >= 2147483648 then u - 4294967296 else u

  sm-i16 : Integer -> Integer
  sm-i16 (u) = if u >= 32768 then u - 65536 else u

  sm-put : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  sm-put (base) (off) (v) = poke-32 base off v

  sm-get : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  sm-get (base) (off) = sm-signed (peek-32 base off)

  sm-get-lo16 : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  sm-get-lo16 (base) (off) = sm-i16 (bit-and (peek-32 base off) 65535)

Section: Loading

 The page copies the file in and calls this. Nothing is trusted: a wrong magic,
 a version this chapter does not know, a file past the window, or a star count
 that does not fit the bytes delivered are each a numbered refusal rather than
 a plausible sky drawn from noise.

  sm-err-none : Integer = 0

  sm-err-magic : Integer = 1

  sm-err-version : Integer = 2

  sm-err-too-big : Integer = 3

  sm-err-truncated : Integer = 4

 "STAR" read as one little-endian word, which is what peek-32 answers over the
 first four bytes. Verified against the shipped file rather than derived.

  sm-magic-star : Integer = 1380013139

  sm-load : Integer -> Integer
  sm-load (len) =
    if len > sm-dat-max then sm-fail sm-err-too-big
    else if sm-get sm-dat-base sm-hdr-magic /= sm-magic-star then sm-fail sm-err-magic
    else if sm-get sm-dat-base sm-hdr-version /= 2 then sm-fail sm-err-version
    else let n = sm-get sm-dat-base sm-hdr-stars
    in if sm-hdr-size + n * sm-rec-size > len then sm-fail sm-err-truncated
    else sm-accept n len

  sm-fail : Integer -> Integer
  sm-fail (code) =
    let a = sm-put 0 sm-cell-error code
    in let b = sm-put 0 sm-cell-ready 0
    in let c = sm-put 0 sm-cell-stars 0
    in 0 - code

  sm-accept : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  sm-accept (n) (len) =
    let a0 = sm-put 0 sm-cell-error sm-err-none
    in let a1 = sm-put 0 sm-cell-stars n
    in let a2 = sm-put 0 sm-cell-datlen len
    in let a3 = sm-put 0 sm-cell-named (sm-get sm-dat-base sm-hdr-named)
    in let a4 = sm-put 0 sm-cell-nameoff (sm-get sm-dat-base sm-hdr-nameoff)
    in let a5 = sm-put 0 sm-cell-dsocount (sm-get sm-dat-base sm-hdr-dsocount)
    in let a6 = sm-put 0 sm-cell-dsooff (sm-get sm-dat-base sm-hdr-dsooff)
    in let a7 = sm-write-cam camera-new
    in let a8 = sm-put 0 (sm-cam-state + sm-cam-objects) n
    in let a9 = sm-put 0 (sm-cam-state + sm-cam-mag-limit) 6000
    in let b0 = sm-put 0 (sm-cam-state + sm-cam-fov) 60
    in let b1 = sm-put 0 (sm-cam-state + sm-cam-selected) sm-absent
    in let b2 = sm-put 0 (sm-cam-state + sm-cam-flags) 7
    in let vis = sm-select
    in let rdy = sm-put 0 sm-cell-ready 1
    in n

Section: The Query

 The whole catalogue is in memory and the page must not walk it every frame,
 so the selection is done here and the page is handed indices. It is rerun
 when the magnitude limit moves, not per frame: the sky does not change.

  sm-star-at : Integer -> Integer
  sm-star-at (i) = sm-dat-base + sm-hdr-size + i * sm-rec-size

  sm-star-mag : Integer -> Integer
  sm-star-mag (i) = sm-get-lo16 (sm-star-at i) sm-rec-magword

  sm-select : Integer
  sm-select =
    let n = sm-get 0 sm-cell-stars
    in let lim = sm-get 0 (sm-cam-state + sm-cam-mag-limit)
    in let v = sm-select-loop 0 n lim 0
    in let w = sm-put 0 sm-cell-visible v
    in v

  sm-select-loop : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  sm-select-loop (i) (n) (lim) (out) =
    if i >= n then out
    else if out >= sm-visible-cap then out
    else if sm-star-mag i > lim then sm-select-loop (i + 1) n lim out
    else let w = sm-put sm-visible-buf (out * 4) i
    in sm-select-loop (i + 1) n lim (out + 1)

Section: Camera State

 Offsets within the camera block. A sky has no camera DISTANCE: zoom is the
 field of view, and the focus is where the observer stands, which only matters
 for parallax against the nearer stars.

  sm-cam-focus-x : Integer = 0

  sm-cam-focus-y : Integer = 8

  sm-cam-focus-z : Integer = 16

  sm-cam-yaw : Integer = 24

  sm-cam-pitch : Integer = 28

  sm-cam-selected : Integer = 40

  sm-cam-objects : Integer = 44

  sm-cam-mag-limit : Integer = 48

  sm-cam-speed : Integer = 52

  sm-cam-fov : Integer = 56

  sm-cam-flags : Integer = 60

  sm-wide-split : Integer = 1000000

  sm-put-wide : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  sm-put-wide (base) (off) (v) =
    let hi = v / sm-wide-split
    in let lo = v - hi * sm-wide-split
    in let w = sm-put base off lo
    in sm-put base (off + 4) hi

  sm-get-wide : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  sm-get-wide (base) (off) = sm-get base off + sm-get base (off + 4) * sm-wide-split

  sm-write-cam : StarCamera -> Integer
  sm-write-cam (cam) =
    let b = sm-cam-state
    in let a0 = sm-put-wide b sm-cam-focus-x (cam.sc-focus-x)
    in let a1 = sm-put-wide b sm-cam-focus-y (cam.sc-focus-y)
    in let a2 = sm-put-wide b sm-cam-focus-z (cam.sc-focus-z)
    in let a3 = sm-put b sm-cam-yaw (cam.sc-yaw)
    in let a4 = sm-put b sm-cam-pitch (cam.sc-pitch)
    in sm-put b sm-cam-speed (cam.sc-speed)

  sm-read-cam : Integer -> StarCamera
  sm-read-cam (x) = StarCamera {
    sc-focus-x = sm-get-wide sm-cam-state sm-cam-focus-x,
    sc-focus-y = sm-get-wide sm-cam-state sm-cam-focus-y,
    sc-focus-z = sm-get-wide sm-cam-state sm-cam-focus-z,
    sc-yaw = sm-get sm-cam-state sm-cam-yaw,
    sc-pitch = sm-get sm-cam-state sm-cam-pitch,
    sc-dist = 0,
    sc-fov = sm-get sm-cam-state sm-cam-fov,
    sc-near = 1,
    sc-far = 100000000000,
    sc-speed = sm-get sm-cam-state sm-cam-speed,
    sc-target-name = ""
  }

Section: Camera Controls

  sm-orbit : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  sm-orbit (dyaw) (dpitch) = sm-write-cam (camera-orbit (sm-read-cam 0) dyaw dpitch)

  sm-look-at : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  sm-look-at (yaw) (pitch) =
    let clamped = if pitch > 89000 then 89000 else if pitch < -89000 then -89000 else pitch
    in let a = sm-put 0 (sm-cam-state + sm-cam-yaw) yaw
    in sm-put 0 (sm-cam-state + sm-cam-pitch) clamped

 Zoom is the field of view in degrees. 5 is about a finder scope and 120 is
 wider than the eye takes in at once; outside that the projection stops being
 worth looking at rather than becoming wrong.

  sm-fov-min : Integer = 5

  sm-fov-max : Integer = 120

  sm-zoom : Integer -> Integer
  sm-zoom (delta) =
    let f = sm-get 0 (sm-cam-state + sm-cam-fov) + delta
    in let clamped = if f < sm-fov-min then sm-fov-min else if f > sm-fov-max then sm-fov-max else f
    in let w = sm-put 0 (sm-cam-state + sm-cam-fov) clamped
    in clamped

  sm-move-forward : Integer -> Integer
  sm-move-forward (amount) = sm-write-cam (camera-move-forward (sm-read-cam 0) amount)

  sm-move-right : Integer -> Integer
  sm-move-right (amount) = sm-write-cam (camera-move-right (sm-read-cam 0) amount)

  sm-move-up : Integer -> Integer
  sm-move-up (amount) = sm-write-cam (camera-move-up (sm-read-cam 0) amount)

  sm-set-speed : Integer -> Integer
  sm-set-speed (s) = sm-put 0 (sm-cam-state + sm-cam-speed) s

Section: Selection and Toggles

  sm-select-obj : Integer -> Integer
  sm-select-obj (i) =
    let n = sm-get 0 sm-cell-stars
    in if i < 0 then sm-put 0 (sm-cam-state + sm-cam-selected) sm-absent
    else if i >= n then sm-put 0 (sm-cam-state + sm-cam-selected) sm-absent
    else let w = sm-put 0 (sm-cam-state + sm-cam-selected) i
    in i

  sm-flag-labels : Integer = 1

  sm-flag-grid : Integer = 2

  sm-flag-constellations : Integer = 4

  sm-toggle-flag : Integer -> Integer
  sm-toggle-flag (bit) =
    let f = sm-get 0 (sm-cam-state + sm-cam-flags)
    in let nf = if bit-and f bit == bit then f - bit else f + bit
    in let w = sm-put 0 (sm-cam-state + sm-cam-flags) nf
    in nf

  sm-toggle-labels : Integer
  sm-toggle-labels = sm-toggle-flag sm-flag-labels

  sm-toggle-grid : Integer
  sm-toggle-grid = sm-toggle-flag sm-flag-grid

  sm-toggle-constellations : Integer
  sm-toggle-constellations = sm-toggle-flag sm-flag-constellations

  sm-set-mag-limit : Integer -> Integer
  sm-set-mag-limit (mag) =
    let w = sm-put 0 (sm-cam-state + sm-cam-mag-limit) mag
    in sm-select

Section: Queries

  sm-get-star-count : Integer
  sm-get-star-count = sm-get 0 sm-cell-stars

  sm-get-visible-count : Integer
  sm-get-visible-count = sm-get 0 sm-cell-visible

  sm-get-error : Integer
  sm-get-error = sm-get 0 sm-cell-error

  sm-tick : Integer -> Integer
  sm-tick (frame) = sm-get 0 sm-cell-visible

Section: Keeping the Page's Entries Alive

 Only what the entry act transitively reaches is emitted, so a function the
 page calls and Codex does not would be dropped and its export would name
 nothing. Touching them here is what keeps them in the module, which is also
 what apps/fishtank/FishTankWasm.codex does under this name. It runs with no
 file loaded, so every read it makes answers zero and every write it makes is
 overwritten by sm-load; do not delete it for looking pointless.

  sm-keep-alive : Integer -> Integer
  sm-keep-alive (x) =
    sm-load x + sm-select + sm-tick x + sm-orbit x x + sm-look-at x x + sm-zoom x + sm-move-forward x + sm-move-right x + sm-move-up x + sm-set-speed x + sm-select-obj x + sm-set-mag-limit x + sm-toggle-labels + sm-toggle-grid + sm-toggle-constellations + sm-get-star-count + sm-get-visible-count + sm-get-error

Section: Entry

 The entry act only prepares memory. Nothing can be reported about the
 catalogue here because the page has not delivered it yet, which is the whole
 difference from a module that carries its own data.

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    adv <- __heap-advance sm-heap-floor
    let churn = sm-keep-alive 0
    in let c0 = sm-put 0 sm-cell-ready 0
    in let c1 = sm-put 0 sm-cell-stars 0
    in let c2 = sm-put 0 sm-cell-visible 0
    in let c3 = sm-put 0 sm-cell-error sm-err-none
    in print-line-uni ("StarMap WASM: ready for a catalogue, window " & show (sm-dat-max / 1048576) & " MB, cap " & show sm-visible-cap)
  end

